To: tradesprior@gmail.com

Post-AGI Alpha Hunt =E2=80=94 fresh rabbit holes Executive take

The best new evidence this run is not another obvious AI beneficiary.
It is *revealed-preference
evidence that scarcity is migrating down another layer*: from electricity =
=E2=86=92
gas turbines =E2=86=92 *precision castings / single-crystal hot-section com=
ponents*.
GE Aerospace agreed on September 8 to pay about $11.75B for Consolidated
Precision Products specifically to secure casting capacity and relieve
engine bottlenecks. Reuters describes castings as a tightly concentrated,
technically difficult supply chain; CPP is the third-largest global
precision-casting supplier, supplies roughly a quarter of GE=E2=80=99s need=
s, and
GE expects airfoil demand to rise >30% by 2030. That is an unusually clean
signal that the scarce asset is not assembly capacity but qualified
metallurgical process capacity.
1. Turbine shortage =E2=86=92 single-crystal casting scarcity

*Alpha score: 9.5/10*

AI data-centre developers trying to bypass grid queues with
behind-the-meter generation are now colliding with multi-year turbine lead
times. But simply buying turbine OEMs is becoming consensus. The more
interesting node is below them: rotor forgings, hot-section blades/vanes,
nickel superalloys, directional-solidification furnaces, ceramic-core
expertise, coating, and qualified foundry throughput.

The strongest signal is GE Aerospace=E2=80=99s ~$11.75B CPP acquisition ann=
ounced
September 8. Reuters reports GE is doing this to alleviate persistent
casting bottlenecks and secure critical capacity. A separate Reuters report
calls casting a =E2=80=98black art=E2=80=99 and emphasizes the small number=
 of capable
suppliers. Howmet=E2=80=99s CEO said the scale of capital expansion require=
d to
meet engine-parts demand is challenging, while also noting incremental
demand from turbine components used in AI-powered data centres.

*Candidate exposures:* Howmet Aerospace (HWM) is the obvious public caster
but now carries customer-concentration/vertical-integration risk from GE.
Carpenter Technology (CRS) is more upstream and potentially cleaner:
specialty nickel/cobalt alloys feed hot-section parts, and FY2026 was its
most profitable year ever, with Specialty Alloys operating margin reaching
37.8%; FY2027 operating income guidance implies another 21=E2=80=9325% incr=
ease.
ATI is another specialty-material exposure, but the connection to this
specific bottleneck needs tighter product-level work.

*What is actually interesting:* the acquisition itself gives us a new
kernel primitive: *strategic acquisition premium as bottleneck evidence*.
When a major downstream OEM buys a supplier at a rich multiple to
internalize a production step, treat that as an empirical update to
indispensability
=C3=97 supply-concentration =C3=97 time-to-replicate.

*Falsifiers:* turbine demand falls sharply; new casting capacity ramps
faster than expected; additive manufacturing/substitute processes eliminate
key casting steps; GE redirects CPP primarily toward internal aerospace use
and weakens third-party pricing; HWM/CRS valuation already discounts the
full scarcity cycle.
2. The next quantum bottleneck may be iteration latency, not merely
cryogenic capacity

*Alpha score: 8.8/10*

A new August 19 arXiv paper identifies *thermal cycling time itself* as a
central bottleneck in superconducting-device R&D. Conventional dilution
refrigerators can require a day or more per cooldown; the paper
demonstrates an ultracompact unit completing a full cooldown/warm-up loop
in roughly 1.2 hours unloaded and 2.1 hours in an instrumented qubit
experiment.

This changes the quantum-infrastructure thesis. The scarce unit is not just
=E2=80=98number of dilution refrigerators=E2=80=99; it is *experiment itera=
tions per
fridge-year*.

Bluefors launched a modular platform in March intended to scale toward
hundreds of thousands of qubits, but fast-cycling characterization systems
could become a separate high-throughput pre-production layer. The real
opportunity may sit in components enabling faster cycles: pulse-tube
cryocoolers, cryogenic RF interconnects, automated probe/sample exchange,
low-thermal-mass packaging, multiplexing, and measurement automation.

*Candidate exposures:* public exposure remains awkward because Bluefors and
Quantum Design are private. Sumitomo Heavy Industries deserves work because
SHI Cryogenics supplies pulse-tube cryocoolers; listed
instrumentation/control vendors can benefit indirectly. More important than
names right now is creating a physical_iteration_latency metric across
quantum, biology, materials and chip testing.

*Falsifier:* fault-tolerant roadmaps rapidly converge on architectures not
requiring deep cryogenics, or compact systems remain useful only for tiny
characterization workloads and cannot support relevant device complexity.
3. Instrument traces may be more valuable than instrument output

*Alpha score: 9.1/10*

A brand-new August 24 arXiv paper, *Beyond FAIR Data: Instrument Traces for
Active and Autonomous Scientific Experimentation*, argues that autonomous
science produces a previously under-recorded asset: the complete *experimen=
tal
trajectory*. The authors reconstruct 118,000 timestamped AFM/PFM events and
show that ordinary saved files expose latent calibration states and
decision patterns but miss unsaved tuning, failures, probe/sample identity,
exogenous state and decision rationale.

This strengthens our negative-data thesis substantially. The moat is not
simply proprietary experimental results. It is:

sample trace + instrument trace + decision trace + failed actions +
calibration drift + maintenance state.

Those traces can support predictive maintenance, counterfactual analysis,
operator training and transfer across facilities. In a world of autonomous
labs, the owner of a large instrument fleet accumulates a *machine-behaviou=
r
dataset that model vendors cannot scrape from papers*.

*Candidate exposures:* this makes instrument vendors with installed fleets
and connected software more interesting than identical hardware sold once.
Bruker, Thermo Fisher, Waters, Keysight, KLA, FormFactor, Revvity and
others should be scored on installed_base =C3=97 telemetry_access =C3=97
recurring_service =C3=97 autonomous-control integration, not merely instrum=
ent
revenue.

*New kernel feature:* add trace_capture_fraction and
proprietary_failure_surface to data-moat scoring.

*Falsifier:* customers insist traces remain customer-owned and
non-transferable; standards commoditize schemas without giving vendors
access; AI can learn sufficiently from small local traces such that scale
provides little advantage.
4. Semiconductor scarcity is migrating from chips to purity infrastructure

*Alpha score: 8.3/10*

Air Liquide has now announced multiple 2026 semiconductor investments
explicitly tied to AI/HBM: =E2=82=AC200M in Japan for ultra-pure
nitrogen/oxygen/argon; >$170M supporting SK hynix=E2=80=99s U.S. HBM
advanced-packaging facility; >$160M for on-site ultra-pure hydrogen in
Arizona; and another South Korean advanced-packaging project. These are
long-term, on-site contracts rather than spot commodity-gas demand.

Entegris Q2 2026 adds a second confirmation: revenue reached $883M (+11.5%
YoY), and management attributed double-digit growth across unit- and
capex-related businesses to semiconductor investment. Its product stack
includes deposition materials, ion-implant specialty gases, etch/clean
chemistries, filtration, purification and contamination control.

*Thesis:* as geometry, stacking and heterogeneous integration become
harder, the value of an individual wafer/package rises. This increases the
economic value of avoiding microscopic contamination. value-at-risk per
particle rises with advanced packaging complexity.

This is a *quality-control Jevons effect*: more expensive integrated
packages induce more spending on purity, filtration and yield preservation.

*Candidate exposures:* ENTG is the clearer high-beta public expression; Air
Liquide provides a more diversified long-duration contractual exposure. The
obscure alpha is likely among filtration, gas-delivery, leak-detection,
valve and analytical-instrument suppliers beneath them.

*Falsifier:* semiconductor capex slows; new nodes require less consumable
intensity than expected; customer insourcing or supplier competition
compresses margins; valuation already discounts sustained AI-node growth.
5. AI chip test is shifting from a single step to a growing sequence of
=E2=80=98known-good=E2=80=99 gates

*Alpha score: 8.5/10, but crowding high*

Teradyne=E2=80=99s July technical note says chiplet AI architectures are cr=
eating
more test insertion points because a defect in an expensive integrated
xPU/HBM package destroys much more value than a defect in a simple die.
Testing increasingly occurs at wafer, die, chip-on-wafer/package and system
level.

FormFactor=E2=80=99s July 29 results provide hard confirmation: record $258=
.2M
quarterly revenue, +31.9% YoY, with both HBM and co-packaged-optics demand
contributing. Cohu reported test-cell utilization around 78% exiting Q1 and
raised its AI/HPC addressable-market estimate.

*New formulation:*

test_value =3D package_value =C3=97 defect_propagation_cost =C3=97
number_of_insertion_points.

As chiplets proliferate, AI can increase test intensity *faster than unit
volume*. That is a powerful nonlinear complement.

*Problem:* this is no longer obscure. FormFactor, Advantest, Teradyne and
related names have already rerated sharply. The alpha hunt should move one
layer lower: probe-card materials, MEMS probe fabrication, thermal-control
stages, sockets/contactors, calibration standards, wafer handling and
photonic alignment.

*Falsifier:* yield improves sufficiently that insertion count falls;
built-in self-test reduces external equipment intensity; test capex
front-loads and then normalizes.
6. Physical-AI insurance may become a deployment gate =E2=80=94 and telemet=
ry the
moat

*Alpha score: 8.0/10; very early*

The June 2026 arXiv paper *Insurance of Agentic AI* argues that agentic
systems create exposures spanning cyber, E&O, product liability and
physical harms, and that underwriting increasingly needs dependency
mapping, telemetry and scenario analysis. August reporting says >90% of
insurers=E2=80=99 AI-agent exposure may currently sit inside =E2=80=98silen=
t=E2=80=99 conventional
coverage rather than dedicated AI policies.

For physical AI this creates a potentially powerful three-sided market:

robot telemetry =E2=86=92 measurable risk =E2=86=92 insurability =E2=86=92 =
permission to deploy.

Insurance could therefore become part of the AI-to-atoms interface. The
valuable data is not generic safety benchmarks; it is millions of real
operating hours, near misses, interventions and environment-specific
failure rates.

There are already private startups combining robot compliance and
insurance. Listed exposure is less obvious=E2=80=94which is exactly why thi=
s is
worth mapping now. Candidates may ultimately be insurers, brokers,
testing/certification firms, industrial telematics vendors, or robot OEMs
that retain operating telemetry.

*Kernel addition:* insurability_threshold. Some technology is economically
unusable until expected loss becomes measurable and cover is available at
an acceptable premium.

*Falsifier:* manufacturers self-insure; regulation assigns liability simply
enough that insurance is not a gating constraint; incident rates fall so
quickly that underwriting remains commodity-like.
7. Financing is becoming a second permission layer for AI infrastructure

*Alpha score: 8.2/10*

A September 8 Reuters Breakingviews analysis estimates AI-related debt
issuance reached nearly $500B by early August 2026, about 20% of
higher-rated U.S. issuance, while lenders are becoming more demanding about
permits, leases and project readiness before releasing capital. This
matters because it creates a new distinction:

technical project =E2=89=A0 permitted project =E2=89=A0 financeable project=
 =E2=89=A0 built project.

Our three-clock model should become *four clocks*:

   1. capability time
   2. permission time
   3. financeability time
   4. cash-flow time

Projects with an interconnection agreement, turbine slot, site control and
committed financing are economically different assets from identical
announced MW without them.

*Tradable angle:* infrastructure owners with secured power/site/financing
may deserve scarcity premiums; developers dependent on rolling speculative
financing deserve a discount. Credit spreads may reveal project viability
earlier than equities.

*Falsifier:* capital markets reopen aggressively and guarantees erase
financing differentiation; project cancellations relieve physical
constraints faster than scarcity rents build.
Highest-priority additions to the kernel from this run

   1. *Strategic-acquisition bottleneck signal:* acquisition premium paid
   by downstream firms to internalize scarce suppliers.
   2. *Physical iteration latency:* experiments/hour rather than number of
   instruments.
   3. *Trace moat:* sample + instrument + decision + failure traces.
   4. *Value-at-risk-per-defect:* predicts nonlinear test/purity intensity
   as assemblies become more expensive.
   5. *Insurability threshold:* probability a capability can actually be
   deployed at commercially acceptable liability cost.
   6. *Financeability time:* add financing readiness as a separate clock
   between permission and realized cash flow.

Best rabbit hole for the next pass

Go down the turbine stack below Howmet: *single-crystal casting inputs and
tooling* =E2=80=94 nickel/cobalt superalloys, ceramic cores, vacuum inducti=
on
melting, directional-solidification furnaces, thermal-barrier coatings,
HIP, precision inspection, rotor forgings and machine tools. GE paying
~$11.75B to internalize CPP is the strongest possible clue that something
there is economically mispriced. We should identify every public supplier
one and two levels below CPP/Howmet, then rank by % revenue exposed =C3=97
industry concentration =C3=97 qualification time =C3=97 expansion difficult=
y =C3=97 market
cap.

This run=E2=80=99s highest-conviction conceptual discovery: *M&A can be use=
d as a
sensor for hidden scarcity.* When a sophisticated downstream actor pays
billions to buy a seemingly boring production process rather than waiting
for supplier capacity to expand, the market has revealed where its internal
world model says the physical constraint really lives.

